…and, with unhurried ease, gradually refine them through the Way and virtue, letting them find recreation and repose within this transforming nurture, until they are fully steeped in the nourishing influence of ritual propriety and righteousness. Those who do not comply with your government are to be transformed and guided with ease; those whom your instruction has not transformed are continually to be molded and refined. This is like the spring wind fostering growth: its power to stir and touch all things is inherent within it. When harmony governs the treatment of the people in this way, I see that under succeeding generations they will be as they were under the former kings. How then could any take it upon themselves to act lawlessly and disrupt good government? Ah! Leniency may be attainable, but leniency tempered by regulation is not easily attained. Harmony may be attainable, but harmony achieved with unhurried ease is not easily attained. To understand expedient measures and meet changing circumstances, thereby bringing about harmonious and balanced government—is this not what is expected of you, Jun Chen? Alas! From this I, in my humble understanding, recognize how consummate was Zhou’s treatment of the people of Yin. At first, the Duke of Zhou was charged, in his exalted capacity as Preceptor and Guardian, with keeping them under vigilant restraint. Then Jun Chen was charged with transforming and guiding them through leniency, harmony, and painstaking accommodation. Yet it seems that even then they had not all become fully compliant. By the time of King Kang, three twelve-year periods had passed, and the Duke of Bi was again charged with safeguarding and governing them, bearing responsibility for bringing the undertaking to completion. Only then, after a generation had changed, did their customs begin to shift. Ah! The difficulty of transforming them reveals how deeply the virtue of Yin’s former kings had reached their people. And the fact that, despite their obstinate and fierce dispositions, they ultimately became good subjects reveals the benevolence with which Zhou’s former kings transformed the people.

Book of Odes

Swift flies that falcon;
Its flight reaches heaven;
It also alights and comes to rest here.
Fang Shu has arrived,
With three thousand chariots,
His hosts and shields displaying their might.
Fang Shu leads them;
The gong officers strike…
